# talkey bindings for sxhkd - works under any X11 window manager.
#
#   cp /usr/share/talkey/bindings/sxhkdrc ~/.config/sxhkd/sxhkdrc   (or append)
#   sxhkd &                                                        (if not already running)
#   pkill -USR1 -x sxhkd                                           (reload)
#
# sxhkd binds key RELEASE with a leading '@', which is what makes hold-to-talk
# work: press starts recording, release transcribes and types.
#
# These are keysyms, not keycodes. Find yours with `xev` and read the name in
# parentheses. The names below are a Keychron K10's media keys; yours will
# differ. If a key emits no keysym at all, use the i3 bindings (which take raw
# keycodes) or remap it with xmodmap first.
#
# Two things sxhkd does not do, because it is only a hotkey daemon:
#   - it cannot float or unfocus the HUD window. eww already asks not to be
#     focused, which most window managers honour; if yours steals focus, add a
#     rule for the window titled "Eww - talkeyhud".
#   - it does not start the background helpers. Use the user services:
#       systemctl --user enable --now talkey-hud talkey-norepeat
#     and set TALKEY_NOREPEAT_KEYCODES in ~/.config/talkey/talkey.env.
#     For them to come back at the next login, your session has to reach
#     graphical-session.target - see bindings/README.md.

# --- dictate: hold to talk, release to type (tap = hands-free toggle) ---
XF86Launch5
    talkey dictate press
@XF86Launch5
    talkey dictate release

# --- dictate in your second language ---
# Which language is TALKEY_LANG2 in ~/.config/talkey/talkey.env, so it is named
# once rather than repeated in every binding file.
XF86Tools
    talkey dictate-alt press
@XF86Tools
    talkey dictate-alt release

# --- memo, translate toggle, double-tap to restart ---
XF86Launch6
    talkey memo press
@XF86Launch6
    talkey memo release

# --- submit: Enter, or cycle the pending action mid-dictation ---
XF86Launch7
    talkey submit

# --- the key cheat sheet ---
super + c
    talkey help
